Here are the LaTeX definitions for the terms used in the **Mean Reciprocal Distance** metric.

This metric provides a normalized score between 0 and 1, where **1** indicates every infected individual was sequenced, and **0** indicates no infected individuals were connected to a sequenced sample.

```latex
\textbf{Metric Definition:}
$$ \text{Coverage Score} = \frac{1}{|P_t|} \sum_{u \in P_t} \frac{1}{d(u, S) + 1} $$

\textbf{Term Definitions:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $P_t$: The set of all infected individuals at time $t$ (the total infected population).
    \item $u$: An individual node within the set $P_t$.
    \item $S$: The subset of individuals selected for genomic sequencing ($S \subseteq P_t$).
    \item $d(u, S)$: The shortest path distance (number of transmission hops) in the transmission graph from individual $u$ to the \textit{nearest} sampled individual in $S$. Mathematically, $d(u, S) = \min_{s \in S} \text{dist}(u, s)$.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If $u \in S$, then $d(u, S) = 0$ (the term becomes $1/(0+1) = 1$).
        \item If $u$ is in a component with no samples (disconnected from $S$), then $d(u, S) = \infty$ (the term approaches $0$).
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
